      "topic_id": "078c6572-75b2-47d5-a080-be4d379653d8",
      "text": "A board-foot problem gives the length as 144 inches. What length should be used in the formula?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "12 feet"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "144 feet"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "1 foot"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "27 feet"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "a",
      "correct_value": null,
      "accepted_answers": null,
      "value_tolerance": 0,
      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "Length must be in feet. 144 inches divided by 12 is 12 feet.",
      "explanation": "Incorrect math or use of a non-standard divisor. This is the NOMINAL BF: (1 × 6 × 200) / 12 = 100 BF. The question asks for actual volume based on the 0.75 x 5.5 dims provided. Gross overestimation. BF = (Actual T × Actual W × Total Linear Feet) / 12 = (0.75 × 5.5 × 200) / 12 = 825 / 12 = 68.75 BF. Linear feet (LF) is simply the total length (L). Use it as the L variable in the BF formula.",
      "code_citation": "NSCP 2015 Section 2401; Quantity Surveying Standards",
      "bloom_level": "apply",
      "application_context": "code_interpretation",
      "practice_context": [],
      "difficulty": 0.7,
      "difficulty_rating": null,
      "discrimination_rating": null,
      "is_assessment": false,
